NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000027 "__END__"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000028 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000029 "=head1 NAME"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000030 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000031 "Digest::SHA1 - Perl interface to the SHA-1 algorithm"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000032 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000033 "=head1 SYNOPSIS"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000034 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000035 " # Functional style"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000036 " use Digest::SHA1  qw(sha1 sha1_hex sha1_base64);"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000037 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000038 " $digest = sha1($data);"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000039 " $digest = sha1_hex($data);"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000040 " $digest = sha1_base64($data);"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000041 " $digest = sha1_transform($data);"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000042 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000043 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000044 " # OO style"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000045 " use Digest::SHA1;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000046 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000047 " $sha1 = Digest::SHA1->new;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000048 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000049 " $sha1->add($data);"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000050 " $sha1->addfile(*FILE);"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000051 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000052 " $sha1_copy = $sha1->clone;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000053 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000054 " $digest = $sha1->digest;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000055 " $digest = $sha1->hexdigest;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000056 " $digest = $sha1->b64digest;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000057 " $digest = $sha1->transform;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000058 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000059 "=head1 DESCRIPTION"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000060 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000061 "The C<Digest::SHA1> module allows you to use the NIST SHA-1 message"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000062 "digest algorithm from within Perl programs.  The algorithm takes as"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000063 "input a message of arbitrary length and produces as output a 160-bit"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000064 "\"fingerprint\" or \"message digest\" of the input."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000065 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000066 "In 2005, security flaws were identified in SHA-1, namely that a possible"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000067 "mathematical weakness might exist, indicating that a stronger hash function"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000068 "would be desirable.  The L<Digest::SHA> module implements the stronger"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000069 "algorithms in the SHA family."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000070 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000071 "The C<Digest::SHA1> module provide a procedural interface for simple"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000072 "use, as well as an object oriented interface that can handle messages"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000073 "of arbitrary length and which can read files directly."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000074 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000075 "=head1 FUNCTIONS"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000076 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000077 "The following functions can be exported from the C<Digest::SHA1>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000078 "module.  No functions are exported by default."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000079 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000080 "=over 4"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000081 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000082 "=item sha1($data,...)"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000083 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000084 "This function will concatenate all arguments, calculate the SHA-1"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000085 "digest of this \"message\", and return it in binary form.  The returned"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000086 "string will be 20 bytes long."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000087 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000088 "The result of sha1(\"a\", \"b\", \"c\") will be exactly the same as the"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000089 "result of sha1(\"abc\")."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000090 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000091 "=item sha1_hex($data,...)"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000092 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000093 "Same as sha1(), but will return the digest in hexadecimal form.  The"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000094 "length of the returned string will be 40 and it will only contain"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000095 "characters from this set: '0'..'9' and 'a'..'f'."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000096 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000097 "=item sha1_base64($data,...)"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000098 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000099 "Same as sha1(), but will return the digest as a base64 encoded string."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000100 "The length of the returned string will be 27 and it will only contain"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000101 "characters from this set: 'A'..'Z', 'a'..'z', '0'..'9', '+' and"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000102 "'/'."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000103 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000104 "Note that the base64 encoded string returned is not padded to be a"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000105 "multiple of 4 bytes long.  If you want interoperability with other"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000106 "base64 encoded sha1 digests you might want to append the redundant"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000107 "string \"=\" to the result."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000108 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000109 "=item sha1_transform($data)"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000110 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000111 "Implements the basic SHA1 transform on a 64 byte block. The $data"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000112 "argument and the returned $digest are in binary form. This algorithm"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000113 "is used in NIST FIPS 186-2"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000114 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000115 "=back"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000116 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000117 "=head1 METHODS"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000118 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000119 "The object oriented interface to C<Digest::SHA1> is described in this"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000120 "section.  After a C<Digest::SHA1> object has been created, you will add"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000121 "data to it and finally ask for the digest in a suitable format.  A"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000122 "single object can be used to calculate multiple digests."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000123 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000124 "The following methods are provided:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000125 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000126 "=over 4"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000127 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000128 "=item $sha1 = Digest::SHA1->new"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000129 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000130 "The constructor returns a new C<Digest::SHA1> object which encapsulate"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000131 "the state of the SHA-1 message-digest algorithm."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000132 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000133 "If called as an instance method (i.e. $sha1->new) it will just reset the"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000134 "state the object to the state of a newly created object.  No new"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000135 "object is created in this case."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000136 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000137 "=item $sha1->reset"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000138 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000139 "This is just an alias for $sha1->new."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000140 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000141 "=item $sha1->clone"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000142 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000143 "This a copy of the $sha1 object. It is useful when you do not want to"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000144 "destroy the digests state, but need an intermediate value of the"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000145 "digest, e.g. when calculating digests iteratively on a continuous data"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000146 "stream.  Example:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000147 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000148 "    my $sha1 = Digest::SHA1->new;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000149 "    while (<>) {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000150 "\t$sha1->add($_);"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000151 "\tprint \"Line $.: \", $sha1->clone->hexdigest, \"\\n\";"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000152 "    }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000153 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000154 "=item $sha1->add($data,...)"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000155 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000156 "The $data provided as argument are appended to the message we"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000157 "calculate the digest for.  The return value is the $sha1 object itself."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000158 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000159 "All these lines will have the same effect on the state of the $sha1"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000160 "object:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000161 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000162 "    $sha1->add(\"a\"); $sha1->add(\"b\"); $sha1->add(\"c\");"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000163 "    $sha1->add(\"a\")->add(\"b\")->add(\"c\");"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000164 "    $sha1->add(\"a\", \"b\", \"c\");"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000165 "    $sha1->add(\"abc\");"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000166 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000167 "=item $sha1->addfile($io_handle)"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000168 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000169 "The $io_handle will be read until EOF and its content appended to the"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000170 "message we calculate the digest for.  The return value is the $sha1"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000171 "object itself."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000172 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000173 "The addfile() method will croak() if it fails reading data for some"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000174 "reason.  If it croaks it is unpredictable what the state of the $sha1"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000175 "object will be in. The addfile() method might have been able to read"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000176 "the file partially before it failed.  It is probably wise to discard"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000177 "or reset the $sha1 object if this occurs."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000178 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000179 "In most cases you want to make sure that the $io_handle is in"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000180 "C<binmode> before you pass it as argument to the addfile() method."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000181 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000182 "=item $sha1->add_bits($data, $nbits)"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000183 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000184 "=item $sha1->add_bits($bitstring)"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000185 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000186 "This implementation of SHA-1 only supports byte oriented input so you"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000187 "might only add bits as multiples of 8.  If you need bit level support"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000188 "please consider using the C<Digest::SHA> module instead.  The"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000189 "add_bits() method is provided here for compatibility with other digest"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000190 "implementations.  See L<Digest> for description of the arguments that"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000191 "add_bits() take."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000192 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000193 "=item $sha1->digest"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000194 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000195 "Return the binary digest for the message.  The returned string will be"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000196 "20 bytes long."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000197 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000198 "Note that the C<digest> operation is effectively a destructive,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000199 "read-once operation. Once it has been performed, the C<Digest::SHA1>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000200 "object is automatically C<reset> and can be used to calculate another"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000201 "digest value.  Call $sha1->clone->digest if you want to calculate the"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000202 "digest without reseting the digest state."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000203 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000204 "=item $sha1->hexdigest"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000205 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000206 "Same as $sha1->digest, but will return the digest in hexadecimal"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000207 "form. The length of the returned string will be 40 and it will only"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000208 "contain characters from this set: '0'..'9' and 'a'..'f'."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000209 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000210 "=item $sha1->b64digest"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000211 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000212 "Same as $sha1->digest, but will return the digest as a base64 encoded"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000213 "string.  The length of the returned string will be 27 and it will only"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000214 "contain characters from this set: 'A'..'Z', 'a'..'z', '0'..'9', '+'"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000215 "and '/'."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000216 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000217 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000218 "The base64 encoded string returned is not padded to be a multiple of 4"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000219 "bytes long.  If you want interoperability with other base64 encoded"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000220 "SHA-1 digests you might want to append the string \"=\" to the result."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000221 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000222 "=back"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000223 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000224 "=head1 SEE ALSO"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000225 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000226 "L<Digest>, L<Digest::HMAC_SHA1>, L<Digest::SHA>, L<Digest::MD5>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000227 ""
